{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And you shall make a parochet of techelet, etc.</b> The parochet, which separates between the Holy and the Holy of Holies, differs in three things from the masach, which separates between the Tent of Meeting and the court of the Mishkan: the parochet was the work of a weaver of cherubim and the masach was the work of an embroiderer; the parochet was on four pillars and the masach on five pillars; the parochet was on silver sockets and the masach on copper sockets — because in the Holy of Holies the priest was not permitted to enter except on Yom Kippur, on which Israel is likened to the ministering angels who are called cherubim, for on that very day the material nature is weakened, as though it does not exist, and nothing remains in man except the intellectual, contemplative faculty, which is called ״מַעֲשֵׂה חֹשֵׁב״ (the work of a thinker). Therefore that parochet was מַעֲשֵׂה חֹשֵׁב כְּרוּבִים, so that whoever looks upon this parochet would understand and grasp that he has no permission to enter there except at a time when he resembles cherubim — the work of a thinker. And so the angel is called ״לְבֻשׁ הַבַּדִּים״ (Ezekiel 9:3), like the manner of the High Priest on Yom Kippur, who was clothed in linen and white garments. And therefore it was on silver sockets, for all silver is white, alluding to the forgiveness of Yom Kippur, that ״יַלְבִּינוּ״ — the sins become white as snow; and at that time the priest would minister in the four white garments mentioned in the parashah of אַחֲרֵי מוֹת. And this matter is reasonable to hear, that the appearance of the parochet and its features allude to that which it separates, for whoever looks upon it understands, through its allusions, what it signifies." "But the masach of the Mishkan separates between the place of the altar, which atones for every sin and transgression committed through the five senses — and the source of sin from the primordial serpent — and the Hekhal, the place of the Sanctuary. Therefore the masach was on five pillars and their sockets were copper, and מַעֲשֵׂה רוֹקֵם (the work of an embroiderer), corresponding to the formation of man, as it is written (Psalms 139:15) ״רֻקַּמְתִּי בְּתַחְתִּיּוֹת אָרֶץ״ (I was embroidered in the nethermost parts of the earth). Here then are three causes of sin: first, from the side of the material that is embroidered in the nethermost parts of the earth; second, from the side of the counsel of the serpent; third, from the side of the interweaving of the activity of the five senses. Therefore the form of the masach that stood opposite the altar was מַעֲשֵׂה רוֹקֵם, on five pillars, and their sockets copper. And whoever contemplates the form of the masach will understand and know that from the side of sin no person is given permission to gaze toward the place of the Sanctuary, as it is stated (Isaiah 59:2) ״עֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם הָיוּ מַבְדִּילִין״ — your iniquities have separated, etc. And the Maharai explained the matter in another way by way of allusion, and it is far from the plain sense."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {1 "מַהֲרִי״א (Maharai) resolved as the standard acronym for Rabbi Yom Tov Lipmann Mühlhausen, though in this context it may refer to a different rabbinic authority abbreviated similarly; the identification is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:23:35.242379481Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}